{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788367430,"nonce":"5LqTUIxw0lOtPOZ4wjl3ZkjS9ww=","title":"Run `m1-20260901` recorded **691 `ok:false` steps out of 4,599**. Broken down by code:","message":"```\n325  bad-args          a real mistake\n200  busy              the bench was mid-advance\n164  unread-journal    the guard refusing, exactly as designed\n  2  rwa-game-down     real\n```\n\n**364 of 691 — 53% — are the protocol working correctly.** `busy` means \"retry in a moment\". `unread-journal` is `722c951`'s guard, the one that stops an agent advancing past events it has not read; a run that trips it a lot is a run being *protected*, not a run failing.\n\nEvery one of them is `ok:false` with an `error.code`, identical in shape to a genuine fault. Nothing in the envelope says which kind it is.\n\n\nFound while watching the cockpit (`df378fa`), which paints all 691 the same red — but the cockpit is only the first consumer to make it visible. Any consumer has the same problem, and each one would have to hardcode its own list of \"codes that are really fine\". That list would then drift from the mod's, which is how `927be4f` happened this round.\n\n`ignored_args` (`7382bdd`) already established the pattern: the mod says something about the *kind* of thing that happened rather than leaving the caller to infer it.\n\n\n1. Every `ok:false` envelope carries a **class** alongside its `code` — proposed `error.class`, with at least: `refused` (a precondition the caller can act on), `flow` (`busy`, and anything meaning \"ask again\"), `fault` (something went wrong), `client` (the `rwa-…` family). The names are the worker's to settle; the three-way distinction is the requirement.\n2. `busy` is `flow`, `unread-journal` is `refused`, `bad-args` is `refused`, `rwa-game-down` is `client`. A run's summary can then say \"364 refusals, 327 faults\" instead of \"691 errors\".\n3. The class is derived at the single place envelopes are built, not maintained as a table beside the codes — a table drifts, and this project has already shipped one drift this round.\n4. `accept/4.2-play-loop.py`'s compliance read stops counting `busy` and `unread-journal` toward anything that looks like a failure rate.\n5. The cockpit renders `flow` and `refused` distinctly from `fault`.\n\n\nA post-mortem that reads \"691 errors\" overstates how badly a run went by roughly half, and an agent reading its own transcript to decide whether it is in trouble gets the same wrong answer. The guard firing 164 times is the single strongest piece of evidence that `722c951` was worth building, and it is currently filed under the same heading as things that broke.","files":null}]}